# Frontend AI Engineering - FlyRank

## Project Context

Capstone repository for the FlyRank **Frontend AI Engineering** track. Development is AI-assisted using **Cursor** as the primary IDE.

## Tech Stack

- **Framework:** Next.js 15 App Router + React 19 + TypeScript
- **Styling:** Tailwind CSS v4 with design tokens in `app/globals.css`
- **Deployment:** Vercel preview deployments on every push
- **Runtime:** Node.js LTS
- **Package manager:** npm
- **Version control:** Git with [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/)
- **IDE:** Cursor with project rules in `.cursor/rules/`

## Conventions

### Commits

Use Conventional Commits format:

- `feat:` — new feature
- `fix:` — bug fix
- `docs:` — documentation only
- `chore:` — maintenance, tooling, config
- `refactor:` — code change without feature/fix
- `test:` — adding or updating tests

### Next.js App Router

- Default to **Server Components** in `app/` routes and shared layout
- Add `'use client'` only for interactivity (menus, forms, browser APIs)
- Put server-side data fetching in Server Components or `lib/` utilities
- Use `process.env` for configuration; document vars in `.env.example`

### Styling

- Use Tailwind utility classes and tokens from `app/globals.css`
- Design for **375px** (mobile) and **1280px** (desktop) breakpoints
- Prefer semantic layout classes (`max-w-6xl`, `grid`, `gap-4`) over fixed pixel widths

### Forms

- Use `react-hook-form` + `zod` + `@hookform/resolvers` for all new forms
- Put validation schemas in `lib/schemas/` and infer TypeScript types with `z.infer`
- Trim string inputs in the schema (`.trim()`) before length or format checks

### Accessibility

- Every form control must have a visible `<label htmlFor="...">` linked to a unique `id`
- Surface field errors with `aria-invalid="true"` and `aria-describedby` pointing to the error element
- Never use `alert()` for form success — use an `aria-live` region instead

### AI-Assisted Workflow

- Use Cursor Agent for implementation tasks
- For non-trivial features, start in Plan mode with file paths, constraints, and a verification step before coding
- Review AI-generated code before committing
- Keep rules files up to date as the stack evolves
- Run `npm run lint` and `npm run build` before pushing feature branches
